Using both design audits, here is the complete improvement backlog for CostFlow. Most of it does not need images or new assets from you.
1. Design system foundation
- Define one official color system and remove accidental color variations.
- Standardize surface, border, text, muted text, action, success, warning, and error colors.
- Use warm-tinted shadows consistently; avoid generic gray shadows.
- Define a radius scale: panels, cards, buttons, inputs, tags.
- Define spacing and grid tokens so every page aligns consistently.
- Apply tabular numerals to currency, percentages, token counts, and chart values.
- Standardize decimal display rules across costs, budgets, analytics, and pricing.
Decision needed from you:
- Keep the current warm cream theme, or move fully to neutral gray?
  My recommendation: keep warm cream. #EDEFF0 should not become the primary card color unless we also make the whole app neutral/cool.
2. Typography
- Confirm one product font family for the app.
- Strengthen page headings while reducing the visual noise of small labels.
- Replace excessive uppercase labels with quieter sentence case or refined small caps.
- Improve line-height and text width in forms, cards, empty states, and landing-page sections.
- Use consistent weights: regular body text, medium labels, semibold headings.
Decision needed from you:
- Choose a font direction: Geist, Satoshi, Cabinet Grotesk, or Outfit.
  My recommendation: Geist for the dashboard and product UI because it is clear and excellent for data.
3. Dashboard improvements
- Improve Cost trend chart readability:
  - fewer visible date labels;
  - subtler gridlines;
  - tooltips for exact date/cost;
  - consistent currency precision;
  - clearer zero-data state.
- Refine summary cards:
  - tighter numeric alignment;
  - clearer hierarchy between label and metric;
  - optional small trend comparison where meaningful.
- Improve Budget overview:
  - preserve the vertical budget list;
  - soften or remove the heavy internal scrollbar;
  - standardize budget row spacing;
  - show state through progress-bar color only when meaningful;
  - keep the budget percentage consistent with the top summary metric.
- Make agent tables easier to scan with stronger column hierarchy and cleaner row hover states.
- Ensure every role dashboard uses the same visual components but only sees authorized data.
No assets needed.
4. Tables, forms, and settings
- Standardize table headers, row height, hover state, selected state, and empty state.
- Make destructive actions consistently red and require clear confirmation.
- Use inline validation for forms instead of vague general errors.
- Add visible keyboard focus states to every interactive element.
- Improve long settings forms with grouped sections and persistent save feedback.
- Replace generic loading spinners with layout-shaped skeleton loaders.
- Ensure modals fit cleanly on smaller laptop screens and mobile devices.
No assets needed.
5. Navigation and responsive behavior
- Keep the floating sidebar—it is a strong part of the current identity.
- Improve sidebar text contrast and hover/pressed feedback.
- Add a compact/collapsed sidebar experience for narrower laptop widths.
- Verify every dashboard role at desktop, tablet, and mobile widths.
- Keep current-page navigation states clear across all roles.
- Add a clear return path from login, sign-up, error, and empty-state screens.
Decision needed from you:
- Should the sidebar collapse to icons on medium screens, or become a slide-out menu on mobile?
  My recommendation: icon-only collapse on tablet/laptop; slide-out menu on mobile.
6. Interaction polish
- Add 200–300ms transitions to buttons, cards, table rows, tabs, and progress bars.
- Add subtle pressed feedback to buttons.
- Add small entrance transitions for dashboard panels.
- Respect reduced-motion preferences.
- Avoid heavy GSAP effects inside dashboards; operational screens should feel fast and stable.
No assets needed.
7. Landing page
- Make the hero more visually distinctive while keeping the heading to two or three lines.
- Keep the existing hero.png as the central brand visual.
- Replace plain feature cards with product-specific visual stories:
  - request routing through the proxy;
  - semantic cache hit;
  - budget enforcement;
  - analytics and savings.
- Use an intentional bento/stack layout with no empty grid space.
- Add restrained scroll motion: reveal, scale/fade media, or stacked cards.
- Improve the final call-to-action and footer, including Privacy and Terms links.
- Remove generic labels and vague AI marketing language.
Assets useful, but optional:
- 2–4 product screenshots, ideally of proxy analytics, budgets, cache savings, and alerts.
- A preferred illustration direction if you do not want screenshots.
- Final marketing copy or a rough description of CostFlow’s target customer.
I would prefer using real CostFlow screens over stock images. It makes the landing page more credible.
8. Empty, error, loading, and accessibility states
- Design friendly empty states for no agents, no budgets, no data, and no alerts.
- Make provider/proxy errors actionable and specific.
- Add skeleton loading states.
- Ensure accessible contrast, focus rings, semantic headings, image alt text, and a skip-to-content link.
- Add a branded 404 page.
- Add legal links before public deployment.
No images required; a small custom illustration set is optional.
9. Branding details
- Confirm favicon, browser title, social preview image, and meta description.
- Use one consistent icon family and stroke width.
- Avoid default-looking icons where custom CostFlow symbols would communicate better.
- Add restrained texture or grain only to marketing backgrounds—not data screens.
Assets needed eventually:
- Square favicon.
- Open Graph/social share image.
- Optional small brand pattern or illustration set.
What I need from you before implementation
Only these decisions are important:
1. Keep the warm cream theme, or switch fully to a neutral/cool theme?
2. Approve Geist as the primary UI font, or choose Satoshi, Cabinet Grotesk, or Outfit.
3. Choose responsive sidebar behavior: icon-collapse plus mobile drawer is my recommendation.
4. Decide whether landing-page visuals should use real product screenshots or custom illustrations.
5. Provide a favicon and social sharing image later, if available.
6. Tell me whether you want the next work phase to prioritize:
   - dashboard polish;
   - landing page;
   - responsive/mobile;
   - states and accessibility.
Everything else can be implemented with the current logo, hero.png, and product UI.